  "description": "Discontinued, superseded by Gen5. This page is kept as an archive record for owners and for anyone researching an older Cotic BFe.\n\nYears of production: 2014-2016\n\nThe BFe 275 was the first 27.5\" BFe. Its 26\" wheeled brother was the BFe 26: see the BFe archive, and the current BFe.\n\nThe BFe 275 is the tearaway of the Cotic family: a rowdy, fast, fun machine.\n\nThe 2016 Cotic BFe 275 had stealth/internal dropper seatpost routing and a Syntace X-12 142x12 thru axle option.\n\nLike its 26\" wheel brother, the BFe 275 liked a more, shall we say, direct approach.\n\nThe BFe 275 was a mighty fine bold-as-brass, rag around play bike. Run it with a 100mm-120mm fork for berm smashing at the 4X track. You could plug in a 160mm fork and send it down the Megavalanche. With 140-150mm it was your trail bike with a bit of beef, showing up some of those fancy pants full suspension bikes at your local Enduro. It was strong as hell and laugh out loud fun, whilst still being lighter than most full suspension bikes.\n\nAll mountain, Enduro, 4X, trail centre, downhill, jumping: the BFe 275 would do it all.\n\nThe frame is based around a Reynolds 853 down tube, which allows us to keep massive strength where it matters most, whilst keeping weight down. The rest of the frame is our own Fm heat treated double butted cromoly steel, which helps keep strength and value high throughout. There are no corners cut: all the usual Cotic features of Ovalform top tube, oversized plug welded seat tube, wishbone stays, cowled dropouts and a taper steerer compatible head tube are present and correct.\n\n27.5\" wheels bring a bit of that 29er style easy rolling performance compared to the 26\" wheels of its older brother, the BFe 26. They aren't hugely different though, so the frame still packages well for shorter riders, and they're still really strong and stiff wheels for getting rowdy with. They're just a little smoother, a little easier to weight when turning into corners, a little more lively and fun.",
